WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:07.766
NARRATOR: Begin by framing Module Structure
and IR Representation as a question about

2
00:00:07.766 --> 00:00:10.734
inputs, decisions, and observable results.

3
00:00:10.734 --> 00:00:17.546
NARRATOR: A module contains global values,
functions, declarations, and metadata.

4
00:00:17.546 --> 00:00:25.214
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: An LLVM
module groups all global variables, function

5
00:00:25.214 --> 00:00:31.703
definitions, and type declarations into a
single unit before compilation begins.

6
00:00:31.703 --> 00:00:38.969
NARRATOR: On screen, display a hierarchical
diagram showing a module node branching into

7
00:00:38.969 --> 00:00:46.234
global values, function nodes, and metadata
nodes, with clear labels for each component.

8
00:00:46.234 --> 00:00:52.429
NARRATOR: List the givens before choosing a
method, because an unstated assumption can

9
00:00:52.429 --> 00:00:53.859
change the conclusion.

10
00:00:53.859 --> 00:00:59.578
NARRATOR: This establishes the vocabulary and
scope needed for the remaining sections.

11
00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:07.155
NARRATOR: Now define Basic Blocks and Control
Flow precisely enough that another learner

12
00:01:07.155 --> 00:01:09.452
could test the definition.

13
00:01:09.452 --> 00:01:16.350
NARRATOR: A function contains basic blocks,
and every basic block ends with a terminator

14
00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:16.843
instruction.

15
00:01:16.843 --> 00:01:22.930
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A
conditional branch in a function splits into

16
00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:29.953
two basic blocks, each terminating with a jump
instruction that routes to the next logical

17
00:01:29.953 --> 00:01:30.421
step.

18
00:01:30.421 --> 00:01:37.247
NARRATOR: On screen, render a flowchart of a
function with three basic blocks, highlighting

19
00:01:37.247 --> 00:01:44.073
the terminator instruction at the end of each
block and arrows indicating control flow

20
00:01:44.073 --> 00:01:44.561
paths.

21
00:01:44.561 --> 00:01:51.276
NARRATOR: Separate the formal definition from
an implementation detail, then identify what

22
00:01:51.276 --> 00:01:53.514
evidence would distinguish them.

23
00:01:53.514 --> 00:01:59.905
NARRATOR: A precise definition makes later
examples easier to evaluate and revise.

24
00:02:00.026 --> 00:02:06.399
NARRATOR: With the foundation in place,
examine how Phi Instructions and Value

25
00:02:06.399 --> 00:02:09.585
Selection operates in a concrete sequence.

26
00:02:09.585 --> 00:02:16.176
NARRATOR: Phi instructions select values
according to the predecessor control-flow

27
00:02:16.176 --> 00:02:16.835
edge.

28
00:02:16.835 --> 00:02:23.132
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: When two
branches converge, a phi instruction chooses

29
00:02:23.132 --> 00:02:28.460
the value from the branch that was actually
executed during runtime.

30
00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:34.900
NARRATOR: On screen, show a diamond-shaped
control flow graph with a phi node at the

31
00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:39.194
merge point, labeled with the values from each
incoming edge.

32
00:02:39.194 --> 00:02:46.304
NARRATOR: Follow every arrow in order and name
the state before and after each transition.

33
00:02:46.304 --> 00:02:51.975
NARRATOR: The trace converts an abstract
statement into an inspectable sequence.

34
00:02:52.082 --> 00:02:59.049
NARRATOR: Next, compare the choices involved
in Explicit Memory Operations and make the

35
00:02:59.049 --> 00:03:00.657
decision criteria explicit.

36
00:03:00.657 --> 00:03:05.641
NARRATOR: Load and store instructions make
memory access explicit.

37
00:03:05.641 --> 00:03:12.706
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A store
instruction assigns a computed value to a

38
00:03:12.706 --> 00:03:18.761
specific memory location, while a load
instruction retrieves that value for further

39
00:03:18.761 --> 00:03:19.266
computation.

40
00:03:19.266 --> 00:03:26.089
NARRATOR: On screen, illustrate a memory model
with a register, a memory address, and arrows

41
00:03:26.089 --> 00:03:31.547
showing a store writing to memory and a load
reading from it.

42
00:03:31.547 --> 00:03:38.260
NARRATOR: Compare alternatives against the
same constraints so the evaluation does not

43
00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:39.938
move the goalposts.

44
00:03:39.938 --> 00:03:46.860
NARRATOR: Using shared criteria turns a
preference into a defensible technical choice.

45
00:03:46.970 --> 00:03:53.515
NARRATOR: Apply Structural Verification Rules
to a worked situation instead of treating it

46
00:03:53.515 --> 00:03:55.529
as an isolated definition.

47
00:03:55.529 --> 00:04:00.857
NARRATOR: Valid IR must satisfy structural and
typing rules.

48
00:04:00.857 --> 00:04:06.958
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: The
verifier checks that every basic block ends

49
00:04:06.958 --> 00:04:13.529
with a terminator and that all variables are
properly typed before the compiler proceeds.

50
00:04:13.529 --> 00:04:20.144
NARRATOR: On screen, display a checklist
graphic with three boxes: structural rules,

51
00:04:20.144 --> 00:04:27.310
typing rules, and verifier output, showing a
red 'X' on a rule violation.

52
00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:33.603
NARRATOR: Work from the concrete inputs to the
result, checking units, types, or invariants

53
00:04:33.603 --> 00:04:34.951
at each step.

54
00:04:34.951 --> 00:04:40.060
NARRATOR: The worked case shows both how to
apply the method and how to check it.

55
00:04:40.178 --> 00:04:46.516
NARRATOR: Examine the tradeoffs in Compiler
Work Separation, including what improves and

56
00:04:46.516 --> 00:04:48.628
what becomes more difficult.

57
00:04:48.628 --> 00:04:56.351
NARRATOR: Compiler work separates parsing and
semantic analysis from lowering, verification,

58
00:04:56.351 --> 00:04:59.862
optimization, and target-specific code
generation.

59
00:04:59.862 --> 00:05:06.210
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: The
parser converts source code into an abstract

60
00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:12.557
syntax tree, while the semantic analyzer
checks types and scopes before the lowering

61
00:05:12.557 --> 00:05:13.534
phase begins.

62
00:05:13.534 --> 00:05:21.629
NARRATOR: On screen, draw a linear pipeline
diagram with five stages labeled parsing,

63
00:05:21.629 --> 00:05:27.855
semantic analysis, lowering, verification,
optimization, and code generation, with arrows

64
00:05:27.855 --> 00:05:30.346
flowing left to right.

65
00:05:30.346 --> 00:05:36.835
NARRATOR: State the benefit, the cost, and the
condition under which the tradeoff is

66
00:05:36.835 --> 00:05:37.299
acceptable.

67
00:05:37.299 --> 00:05:44.737
NARRATOR: Making the tradeoff visible prevents
a local improvement from hiding a larger cost.

68
00:05:44.858 --> 00:05:51.904
NARRATOR: Stress-test the reasoning around
Lexical Analysis and Scoping with an edge case

69
00:05:51.904 --> 00:05:55.698
and identify which assumption controls the
outcome.

70
00:05:55.698 --> 00:06:00.636
NARRATOR: Names are bound to objects within
namespaces and scopes.

71
00:06:00.636 --> 00:06:06.782
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A
variable declared inside a function belongs to

72
00:06:06.782 --> 00:06:13.401
the function's local scope, while a global
variable is bound to the module's namespace.

73
00:06:13.401 --> 00:06:20.421
NARRATOR: On screen, show nested boxes
representing global and local scopes, with

74
00:06:20.421 --> 00:06:27.441
variable names placed inside the appropriate
scope box and arrows indicating binding

75
00:06:27.441 --> 00:06:28.026
relationships.

76
00:06:28.026 --> 00:06:34.173
NARRATOR: Change one assumption at a time and
observe which part of the reasoning no longer

77
00:06:34.173 --> 00:06:34.557
holds.

78
00:06:34.557 --> 00:06:40.805
NARRATOR: The edge case reveals the boundary
of the claim without discarding the useful

79
00:06:40.805 --> 00:06:41.698
core idea.

80
00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:49.074
NARRATOR: Use Data Model and Execution Model
to diagnose a failure by separating symptoms,

81
00:06:49.074 --> 00:06:51.150
causes, and corrective action.

82
00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:58.353
NARRATOR: Expressions produce values, while
statements control execution and bindings.

83
00:06:58.353 --> 00:07:04.325
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: An
integer object has a unique memory address, a

84
00:07:04.325 --> 00:07:10.723
type tag, and a numeric value, while an
assignment statement binds a name to that

85
00:07:10.723 --> 00:07:11.150
object.

86
00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:18.697
NARRATOR: On screen, render a simple object
diagram showing an object with three fields:

87
00:07:18.697 --> 00:07:26.244
identity (address), type, and value, alongside
an assignment arrow binding a name to it.

88
00:07:26.244 --> 00:07:31.887
NARRATOR: Locate the first point where
observed behavior diverges from expected

89
00:07:31.887 --> 00:07:35.478
behavior, then test the smallest plausible
cause.

90
00:07:35.478 --> 00:07:41.566
NARRATOR: The diagnosis is strongest when
another person can reproduce the observation

91
00:07:41.566 --> 00:07:43.088
and the test.

92
00:07:43.202 --> 00:07:49.304
NARRATOR: Connect Exception Handling and
Control Flow to the earlier ideas and explain

93
00:07:49.304 --> 00:07:52.589
how the pieces work as one system.

94
00:07:52.589 --> 00:07:58.902
NARRATOR: Exceptions interrupt normal control
flow until a matching handler processes them

95
00:07:58.902 --> 00:08:00.480
or they propagate.

96
00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:06.044
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: When a
division by zero occurs, an exception jumps

97
00:08:06.044 --> 00:08:12.801
out of the current block and is caught by a
try-except handler that logs the error and

98
00:08:12.801 --> 00:08:13.198
continues.

99
00:08:13.198 --> 00:08:20.359
NARRATOR: On screen, illustrate a control flow
path with a normal execution line, a red

100
00:08:20.359 --> 00:08:27.042
'exception' arrow jumping to a handler block,
and a green 'propagate' arrow moving upward.

101
00:08:27.042 --> 00:08:35.929
NARRATOR: Explain each connection explicitly
and verify that information retains the same

102
00:08:35.929 --> 00:08:38.151
meaning across representations.

103
00:08:38.151 --> 00:08:45.058
NARRATOR: The synthesis matters only if every
connection remains supported by the original

104
00:08:45.058 --> 00:08:45.589
evidence.

105
00:08:45.698 --> 00:08:50.863
NARRATOR: Finish with Debugging and
Decomposition as a practical checklist that

106
00:08:50.863 --> 00:08:53.210
can guide a new analysis.

107
00:08:53.210 --> 00:09:01.078
NARRATOR: Debugging separates syntax, runtime,
and logical failures, while decomposition and

108
00:09:01.078 --> 00:09:06.085
abstraction reduce the number of interacting
concerns.

109
00:09:06.085 --> 00:09:13.850
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A syntax
error is caught by the parser, a runtime error

110
00:09:13.850 --> 00:09:21.130
occurs during execution, and a logical error
is fixed by breaking the code into smaller,

111
00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:22.101
testable functions.

112
00:09:22.101 --> 00:09:29.320
NARRATOR: On screen, show a three-column table
labeled syntax, runtime, and logical failures,

113
00:09:29.320 --> 00:09:36.538
with each column containing a brief example
and a corresponding debugging tool icon.

114
00:09:36.538 --> 00:09:44.067
NARRATOR: For each checklist item, record the
evidence, the remaining uncertainty, and the

115
00:09:44.067 --> 00:09:45.804
next verification step.

116
00:09:45.804 --> 00:09:51.821
NARRATOR: The result is a repeatable
procedure, not a conclusion that depends on

117
00:09:51.821 --> 00:09:53.210
memory or intuition.
